<methodology>
Backward taint analysis from XML-parse sinks.
1. Sinks. lxml.etree (resolve_entities default True), xml.dom.minidom, xml.sax,
   ElementTree (older), Java DocumentBuilderFactory/SAXParserFactory/XMLInputFactory,
   .NET XmlDocument/XmlTextReader, libxml. Trace the parsed input backward to its source.
2. Sources. Request bodies, uploaded files, SOAP/SVG/Office/SAML payloads, webhooks.
3. Adequate control. External entities AND DOCTYPE/DTD must be explicitly disabled
   (e.g. resolve_entities=False + no_network; FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING / disallow-doctype-decl).
4. Variants. Classic file read via SYSTEM entity, SSRF via external entity, billion-laughs DoS
   via internal entity expansion, parameter entities for OOB exfiltration.
</methodology>

<examples>
Illustrative vulnerable patterns (different languages; NOT specific to this repo —
do not assume these files exist here; use them to recognise the shape of the bug):
  - python: `etree.fromstring(data, etree.XMLParser(resolve_entities=True))`
    why: external entities are resolved on untrusted XML -> file read / SSRF.
  - python: `xml.dom.minidom.parseString(request.data)`
    why: default parser processes DOCTYPE/entities on untrusted input.
  - java: `DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance().newDocumentBuilder().parse(in)`
    why: factory defaults allow DTDs/external entities unless disabled.
</examples>

<false_positives_to_avoid>
- A parser with external-entity resolution and DOCTYPE explicitly disabled is safe.
- Parsing trusted, server-generated XML is lower risk (still note it).
- JSON or non-XML parsing is out of scope for this class.
</false_positives_to_avoid>

Confidence: High = clear unprotected path, deterministic. Medium = strongly
indicated but a material uncertainty remains. Low = plausible but the trace is
incomplete. Rule: when uncertain, round down (favour Medium/Low).

Begin by reading the entry-point files above (or, if none, list_dir the scope),
then trace each candidate toward the sinks above.

